{"entries":{"chapter-30-study-nutrition-disorders-obesity-and-enteral-parenteral-alimentation|Chapter 30 Study: Nutrition Disorders, Obesity, and Enteral/Parenteral Alimentation":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Aging increases the fat-to-body-mass ratio; muscle preservation depends on both diet and activity. Obesity can coexist with serious protein undernutrition (p405–406).":["u3"],"copy|Unintentional loss of at least 5% of baseline weight over 6–12 months warrants investigation (p406).":["u4"],"copy|Assess intake, physical findings, illness, medications and social barriers. Albumin/prealbumin chiefly reflect inflammation and disease severity and are not stand-alone nutritional diagnoses (p406, p425).":["u5"],"copy|Starvation-related losses may reverse with feeding; inflammatory cachexia includes muscle catabolism that refeeding may not reverse (p424).":["u6"],"copy|Investigate causes and support intake early; high-protein/high-calorie oral supplements may reduce morbidity and mortality in hospitalized malnourished patients at least 75 (p406).":["u7"],"copy|Protein RDA is 0.8 g/kg/day; some advocate 1.0–1.25 g/kg/day for older adults, but the chapter describes ongoing uncertainty (p415).":["u8"],"copy|Acutely ill hospitalized patients or those recovering from major surgery/trauma generally warrant 1.5 g/kg/day or more, unless renal/hepatic disease necessitates restriction (p415).":["u9"],"copy|Selected obese older adults may benefit from controlled weight loss, but diet-only weight loss also reduces muscle and bone. Combine nutrition with individualized endurance/resistance exercise (p413–414).":["u10"],"copy|Enteral feeding is preferred when nutritional support is needed and the gut functions; prognosis and preferences remain essential (p406, p429).":["u11"],"copy|Bowel obstruction, ischemia, major upper-GI bleeding, inadequate bowel surface or intractable vomiting/diarrhea can preclude enteral feeding (p429).":["u12"],"copy|Refeeding may shift potassium and phosphate intracellularly; monitor tolerance, daily weight, glucose and electrolytes until stable (p432).":["u13"],"copy|Parenteral glucose should not exceed 5 mg/kg/min; excess delivery risks hyperglycemia, excess carbon dioxide production and fatty liver (p433).":["u14"],"copy|Megestrol can cause thrombosis, fluid retention and increased mortality; observed weight gain is often fat rather than improved function (p428).":["u15"],"copy|Mirtazapine lacks high-quality evidence for weight gain without depression. Routine micronutrient supplements have no demonstrated benefit without deficiency or high deficiency risk (p406, p429).":["u16"],"copy|Low albumin does not prove inadequate feeding, and obesity does not exclude malnutrition (p406, p423).":["u17"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u18"],"copy|2023-Jun Q41":["u19"],"copy|2024-Sep Q91":["u20"],"copy|CHAPTER 30s / HAZZARD":["u21"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 30 - Nutrition Disorders, Obesity, and Enteral/Parenteral Alimentation; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u22"],"copy|Nutrition & assessment":["u23"],"copy|Management":["u24"],"copy|Artificial nutrition":["u25"],"copy|Drugs & exam traps":["u26"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (2):":["u27"],"copy|Two questions below cite passages in Chapter 30 that directly teach their answers.":["u27"]},"maxId":27}
